WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.980
A European service station on the moon will keep experiments running for years by storing solar power for the two-week lunar night. This is Fascinating Frontiers, episode 228. After that lunar hub come Uranus and Neptune's separate origins, twilight clouds over gale crater and a cosmic deep dive on ice giant migration. The outpost for Advanced Scientific Investigation Station, or Oasis, is the European Space Agency designed for that shared surface hub. It will supply at least 75 watts of

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:35.280
continuous power during the lunar day and 15 watts at night, along with communications,

00:00:35.720 --> 00:00:38.380
data handling, and storage for multiple payloads.

00:00:38.820 --> 00:00:44.240
OHB in Germany is leading the feasibility studies, and a systems requirement review is already

00:00:44.240 --> 00:00:44.780
underway.

00:00:45.340 --> 00:00:49.940
The station is modular and extendable, and it could offer sheltered spots where rovers

00:00:49.940 --> 00:00:50.760
weighed out the dark.

00:00:51.300 --> 00:00:57.780
One early payload, the environment characterization and heliophysics observatory, or Echo, will

00:00:57.780 --> 00:01:00.620
We'll test the infrastructure while it studies the lunar environment.

00:01:01.539 --> 00:01:06.899
The layout draws on the Apollo lunar surface experiments package, or ALSEP, which connected

00:01:06.899 --> 00:01:08.639
instruments to a central station.

00:01:09.379 --> 00:01:14.379
Oasis uses a solar array and a separate battery unit, so experiment teams no longer each

00:01:14.379 --> 00:01:16.519
haul their own heavy night survival hardware.

00:01:17.375 --> 00:01:22.855
Future versions could feed science or technology demonstrators farther from the lander, or serve

00:01:22.855 --> 00:01:24.515
as rover garages through the night.

00:01:24.515 --> 00:01:30.535
The concept rides an Argonaut lander launched on a four-booster Ariane 6, with communications

00:01:30.535 --> 00:01:33.555
through the Moonlight Network starting with Lunar Pathfinder.

00:01:34.275 --> 00:01:39.735
ESA wants this as a recurring European lunar capability, not a one-off, scaled so several

00:01:39.735 --> 00:01:41.615
robotic payloads can run at once.

00:01:42.732 --> 00:01:48.392
Uranus and Neptune show different chemical make-ups according to a Fizz.org space report on an

00:01:48.392 --> 00:01:49.752
international team's work.

00:01:49.752 --> 00:01:54.912
The team found Neptune more enriched in oxygen than Uranus by probing the two atmospheres

00:01:54.912 --> 00:01:55.592
indirectly.

00:01:56.072 --> 00:02:00.252
That contrast suggests the ice giants formed in different regions of the early solar

00:02:00.252 --> 00:02:03.132
system before they migrated to their current orbits.

00:02:03.732 --> 00:02:08.092
The result challenges the long-held view that the two planets share a common birthplace.

00:02:08.592 --> 00:02:13.992
By measuring composition through those atmospheric observations, the researchers revealed a significant

00:02:13.992 --> 00:02:15.652
difference in oxygen content.

00:02:15.652 --> 00:02:19.892
models of the primitive solar system must now account for chemically distinct

00:02:19.892 --> 00:02:25.756
formation zones rather than identical starting conditions. Uranus sits 2.9

00:02:25.756 --> 00:02:31.596
billion kilometers from the sun, while Neptune lies another 1.6 billion kilometers farther out.

00:02:32.036 --> 00:02:35.976
That gap is wide enough to contain the entire inner solar system twice over.

00:02:36.536 --> 00:02:41.016
The separation once looked like evidence the two worlds formed as twins in the same region.

00:02:41.656 --> 00:02:44.976
Imagine riding with the building blocks while the young solar system stirred.

00:02:44.976 --> 00:02:45.076
occurred.

00:02:45.576 --> 00:02:48.596
Closer to the sun, temperatures allowed more oxygen-bearing

00:02:48.596 --> 00:02:49.636
ices to condense.

00:02:50.216 --> 00:02:52.336
Farther out, the mixed-favored other compounds.

00:02:52.956 --> 00:02:57.056
A planet assembled in the oxygen-rich zone would carry a different bulk composition

00:02:57.056 --> 00:02:58.716
than one born farther away.

00:02:59.376 --> 00:03:04.616
Once giant planets began flinging one another through gravitational encounters, those original

00:03:04.616 --> 00:03:06.116
locations became scrambled.

00:03:06.716 --> 00:03:11.276
The surviving chemical fingerprints in today's atmospheres are the only record left of where

00:03:11.276 --> 00:03:12.316
each world started.

00:03:12.316 --> 00:03:17.996
The team used indirect atmospheric probing, with no probes sent into the planets themselves.

00:03:18.676 --> 00:03:23.216
We still do not know exactly how far apart those birthplaces were, or what triggered

00:03:23.216 --> 00:03:23.816
the migration.

00:03:24.516 --> 00:03:29.116
Models can reproduce the current orbits, yet they cannot yet match the measured oxygen

00:03:29.116 --> 00:03:32.476
contrast without fine-tuning assumptions about the early disk.

00:03:32.976 --> 00:03:37.676
A single high precision measurement of another heavy element in both atmospheres could decide

00:03:37.676 --> 00:03:42.076
whether the planets formed on opposite sides of a chemical boundary, or whether some

00:03:42.076 --> 00:03:47.576
later process erased and expected similarity. The same data might clarify whether the migration

00:03:47.576 --> 00:03:53.676
happened early, while the disk was still gas-rich, or later after most of the gas had dissipated.

00:03:54.276 --> 00:03:59.136
Either scenario would reshape how we reconstruct the dynamical history of the outer solar system

00:03:59.476 --> 00:04:05.571
and the delivery of volatiles to the inner planets. Curiosity captured zenith movies of wispy

00:04:05.571 --> 00:04:11.491
clouds above gale crater shortly before sunset on Sol 5,037, NASA reports.

00:04:12.171 --> 00:04:17.031
The clouds are bright enough to appear in raw nav cam images, even though most atmospheric

00:04:17.031 --> 00:04:19.311
clouds at the site remain too thin to see.

00:04:19.911 --> 00:04:24.891
The observations mark the start of seasonal twilight cloud activity as Mars enters its northern

00:04:24.891 --> 00:04:25.311
spring.

00:04:25.991 --> 00:04:30.791
The new Mars year began at solar longitude zero degrees at the end of September on Earth.

00:04:30.791 --> 00:04:35.331
The rover wrapped up drill activities at Basque Lakes while collecting additional

00:04:35.331 --> 00:04:41.771
GCMS data and remote sensing observations from MassCam and ChemCAM. The team plans to

00:04:41.771 --> 00:04:46.471
drive back toward crystal forms seen in the Choo Choo region after finishing contact science

00:04:46.471 --> 00:04:52.294
on the drill tailings. South Australia's government reaffirmed support for its astronaut program

00:04:52.294 --> 00:04:58.134
despite shifting timelines, ABC News reports. The commitment keeps open a pathway for an

00:04:58.134 --> 00:05:01.934
Australian of the Year candidate to reach orbit through international partnerships.

00:05:02.434 --> 00:05:07.934
The states outback already hosted sample recoveries from Japan's Hayabusa 2 mission in 2020.

00:05:08.694 --> 00:05:12.994
The ongoing programs strengthen South Australia's role in international space work.

00:05:13.454 --> 00:05:18.694
It also provides openings for Australian researchers in spacecraft recovery and technical operations.

00:05:18.694 --> 00:05:26.667
Euclid detected comet C-2026-S-1, Euclid in images taken between the 16th and 24th of

00:05:26.667 --> 00:05:29.627
September 2026, Universe Today reports.

00:05:30.327 --> 00:05:35.067
The European Space Agency spacecraft was surveying galaxies to study dark matter and

00:05:35.067 --> 00:05:36.867
dark energy when the object appeared.

00:05:37.467 --> 00:05:42.327
Those wide field observations captured the new comet 810 million miles from Earth.

00:05:42.847 --> 00:05:48.087
The fine shows the telescope can pick out solar system objects alongside its primary cosmological

00:05:48.787 --> 00:05:53.560
A stellar mass black hole system has been verified as a micro blazar,

00:05:54.140 --> 00:05:58.560
universe today reports, the galactic counterpart to extra galactic blazar's.

00:05:58.900 --> 00:06:02.820
The object may accelerate the most energetic particles observed in the galaxy.

00:06:03.460 --> 00:06:06.420
Distant blazar's are powered by supermassive black holes.

00:06:06.920 --> 00:06:08.900
This one operates inside the Milky Way.

00:06:09.380 --> 00:06:13.420
That nearby setting offers a closer view of the same high energy processes.

00:06:14.548 --> 00:06:18.368
McGill University astronomers measured infrared emission from gas clouds

00:06:18.368 --> 00:06:23.348
near Sagittarius A-Star, and found elevated levels of elements heavier than helium,

00:06:23.828 --> 00:06:28.768
according to Fizz.org Space. The composition matches stars orbiting the black hole,

00:06:29.268 --> 00:06:34.448
suggesting the gas and stars formed together. The enrichment likely came from earlier generations

00:06:34.448 --> 00:06:39.808
of exploding stars. The James Webb Space Telescopes' high spectral and spatial resolution

00:06:39.808 --> 00:06:44.968
let the team distinguish heat signatures of multiple gas clouds and measure their metal content.

00:06:44.968 --> 00:06:50.648
Nickle Ford, the lead author, noted that understanding why the black hole consumes material

00:06:50.648 --> 00:06:55.788
more slowly requires examining the properties of available gas, dust, and stars.

00:06:56.288 --> 00:07:00.768
A companion study tested whether the gas preserved evidence of previous energy bursts

00:07:00.768 --> 00:07:04.548
from the black hole, but found local conditions dominate the emission.

00:07:05.552 --> 00:07:11.792
Young tectonic landforms on the moon now add up to 10,000 mapped cracks, space.com reports,

00:07:11.792 --> 00:07:15.872
accounts used to measure how much the interior has contracted as it cooled.

00:07:16.472 --> 00:07:20.232
The work gives the clearest picture yet of the moon's ongoing shrinkage.

00:07:20.632 --> 00:07:25.072
Those features formed as the interior lost heat and the crust adjusted to the reduced

00:07:25.072 --> 00:07:25.532
volume.

00:07:26.172 --> 00:07:29.592
The mapping traces that long-term volume loss across the surface.

00:07:30.632 --> 00:07:36.372
Mercury still has no lander, Fizz.org space notes, because extreme temperatures, high orbital

00:07:36.372 --> 00:07:39.312
speeds, and harsh radiation have blocked every attempt.

00:07:39.312 --> 00:07:44.532
• Scientists argue that only a dedicated lander can resolve long-standing questions about

00:07:44.532 --> 00:07:49.172
the planet's composition and history • Reaching the innermost world demands propulsion

00:07:49.172 --> 00:07:53.852
and thermal protection beyond the systems used for more distant targets, such as the

00:07:53.852 --> 00:07:59.025
icy moons of Saturn or Jupiter • A pair of supermassive black holes could collide

00:07:59.025 --> 00:08:04.385
on a time scale as short as 100 years, and EWS.am tech reports

00:08:04.385 --> 00:08:09.185
• Observation suggests that coalescence would be the first predicted merger on human

00:08:09.185 --> 00:08:14.785
time scales. The short interval offers a rare chance to watch the final stages of a supermassive

00:08:14.785 --> 00:08:21.773
black hole merger directly. Spanish startup UARX Space is also today addressing issues identified

00:08:21.773 --> 00:08:28.293
during the first flight of its OSSIED orbital transfer vehicle, launched on the first of October

00:08:28.293 --> 00:08:34.773
aboard a SpaceX transporter mission. A study using ALMA data shows how jets from supermassive black holes

00:08:34.773 --> 00:08:39.473
can heat gas at the outer edges of galaxies and slow star formation over time.

00:08:39.993 --> 00:08:44.393
Before we go, watch for the next word on that Spanish orbital transfer vehicle as the

00:08:44.393 --> 00:08:45.513
troubleshooting continues.

00:08:46.053 --> 00:08:48.293
That covers today's Space and Science news.

